---
scope: src/tools/init/installMethodologyDocs
intent: >
  installMethodologyDocs is the helper that lands the progressively-
  disclosed AIDE methodology hub onto the host project's disk. It reads
  every canonical methodology doc through initContent, writes them into a
  dedicated host-side doc-hub folder, and writes a hub index file
  alongside them that links to each doc by relative path. The sibling
  helper writeMethodology installs only a short pointer stub in the host
  config file; this helper installs the actual material the stub points
  at. Success is a host project where an agent following the stub's
  pointer finds a folder of canonical methodology content identical to
  what lives in `docs/`, organized for progressive disclosure so the
  agent reads only what the current task requires.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every canonical methodology doc that belongs in the host-side hub
      is installed verbatim — byte-faithful with its source under
      `docs/`, no trimming, no reformatting, no paraphrasing, no
      reordered sections. The content an agent reads in the host is
      identical to what the maintainer edits upstream.
    - A hub index file is installed alongside the doc set. The index's
      only job is to name each installed doc and link to it by relative
      path, so an agent following the stub's pointer lands on a file
      that names everything in the hub and crawls from there. The hub
      is the study-playbook pattern applied to the host-side methodology
      surface.
    - Every read of `docs/` goes through initContent. This helper does
      not read the filesystem directly, and it does not hardcode the
      list of methodology docs in its own source — it asks initContent
      which canonical methodology docs exist and reads each one back by
      canonical name. The single-reader invariant the parent spec
      depends on is preserved across this addition.
    - Per-file idempotency. Each canonical doc is written only if the
      target file is missing; existing files are left untouched and
      reported as `exists`. A user who has customized one of the
      installed docs keeps their customization across re-runs. The hub
      index file obeys the same rule.
    - Per-file status reporting. Each install returns its own
      `created`/`exists` status so the init summary can show the mix
      honestly — "aide-spec: created, automated-qa: exists" — without
      collapsing the whole step into a single aggregate line.
    - The helper is composable with failure. A missing or unreadable
      single canonical doc fails that install and surfaces the failure
      in the summary; it does not cascade into the other installs, and
      it does not abort the init run as a whole.
    - The host-side target location is derived from a single source
      that writeMethodology's stub also consults. The stub points at a
      path, this helper writes into that same path, and the shared
      source makes the two agreements impossible to break by editing
      one side without the other.
  undesired:
    - A second reader of `docs/` within the init subtree. This helper
      bypassing initContent to fetch the canonical bytes — even "just
      once, for performance" — breaks the chokepoint invariant, opens
      the door to drift between what methodology reads and what this
      helper reads, and hides that drift from code review.
    - A hardcoded list of methodology doc names inside this helper's
      source, pinning the install set to literals that initContent's
      registry no longer owns. If a canonical doc is added or removed
      in `docs/`, it must propagate through initContent's enumeration
      API; a hardcoded list here would silently omit the new doc or
      try to install a removed one.
    - Overwriting an existing file in the host's doc hub because "the
      canonical version is authoritative". It is authoritative for
      first-time installs only. A developer who has customized a
      methodology doc for their team's workflow keeps that
      customization through re-runs. Silent re-sync belongs to a
      future explicit update path, not to this helper.
    - A single aggregate status for the whole step — "docs installed"
      — that hides which docs were new and which were already in
      place. The init summary depends on per-file granularity to give
      the developer a useful transition report.
    - An install run that aborts the whole helper if one canonical doc
      is unreadable. The other four doc installs could have succeeded
      and this helper's peer steps (command scaffold, MCP wire, IDE
      configuration) should never be blocked by an isolated failure
      here. Each doc is independently installable and independently
      reportable.
    - A target path that disagrees with the path writeMethodology's
      stub names. The stub tells the agent where to crawl; this
      helper creates that crawl target; any disagreement between the
      two sends the agent to an empty directory and destroys trust
      in the tooling. The two sides must derive from a common source,
      not from coincident string literals.
    - A hub index file whose content is composed from doctrine
      literals in this helper's source. The index is small enough to
      feel like "framework plumbing", but any sentence in it that
      teaches an AIDE rule is canonical content and must come from
      `docs/` through initContent. The rendered paths and the
      enclosing structure are plumbing; the teaching is not.
    - Reaching for the network to fetch the canonical docs. The same
      rule the parent locks in applies here: every asset this helper
      installs must already be present on disk from the repo it was
      built out of, because init must succeed offline.
---

## Context

The AIDE methodology is a doc set — five canonical files under `docs/`
— and before this helper existed, the only delivery mechanism for those
files was to concatenate them into a single block inside the host's
config file. That worked while the methodology was small, but the
methodology's own doctrine is progressive disclosure: agents should
read only the part of a doc set their current task actually requires.
Shipping the whole set into a surface the agent reads on every session
was the opposite of the pattern the docs were teaching.

This helper exists to install the methodology the right way: as a
folder of canonical docs sitting on the host project's disk, one file
per canonical name, with a hub index file that names all of them. An
agent following a pointer from the host's config file reads the hub
index first, sees the full list, and crawls into only the specific doc
its task needs. The pattern mirrors what `study-playbook` already does
against the user's global brain — a shallow index that enumerates a
deep set.

The parent spec's chokepoint invariant governs this addition: every
read of `docs/` within the init subtree goes through initContent.
Adding a second reader would be a regression even if the local code
were simpler for it, because the single-reader invariant is what
guarantees that a future change to `docs/` is a one-file edit. This
helper participates in that invariant by asking initContent both for
the content of each methodology doc and for the list of which docs
belong in the methodology set.

## Strategy

**The host-side hub mirrors the canonical shape, flat and verbatim.**
Every canonical methodology doc is written to the host under its own
canonical-name file, with its bytes unmodified from the source in
`docs/`. There is no rewriting of headings, no stripping of
frontmatter, no link rewriting, no composition. A file diff between
the installed hub and the canonical source shows only the presence of
the hub index file; every methodology doc itself is byte-identical.
The reason is the same reason writeMethodology never transforms its
content either: the moment this helper becomes "smart" about the
canonical docs, it becomes a second source of truth and begins to
drift on the first edit upstream.

**The hub index file is a pure map, not a summary.** The installed
hub needs some file the agent lands on first when it follows the
stub's pointer — otherwise the agent has to scan a directory and guess
what order to read things in. That landing file is a short index: one
entry per installed doc, each entry naming the canonical doc and
linking to it by relative path. The index does not summarize the
docs, does not explain the methodology, and does not teach any AIDE
rules of its own — those would be doctrine and would have to come
from canonical content. The index is pure structure.

**Enumeration lives in initContent, not here.** The list of which
canonical docs belong to the methodology set is the kind of decision
that must live alongside the name-to-path registry initContent
already owns. If a canonical doc is added or removed, the registry
change and the methodology-membership change are the same change, and
they land in the same file. This helper asks initContent for the
enumeration at call time and iterates whatever list comes back.
Hardcoding the list locally would double the surface at which a
canonical-doc change has to land, and the second landing site would
always be the one that got missed.

**Per-file idempotency, per-file reporting.** The pattern is exactly
the one scaffoldCommands already uses for the pipeline slash
commands: iterate the list, check whether the target file exists,
either write it and report `created` or skip it and report `exists`.
No aggregation, no fail-fast, no silent overwrite. The init summary
renders the per-file statuses directly. This design is a conscious
clone of a helper that already works; the consistency across
installers matters more than any micro-optimization either could make
on its own.

**Target path derived from a shared source.** The host-side hub path
and the path writeMethodology names in its stub must agree by
construction. This helper and writeMethodology both read the path
from a single shared constant or helper — whichever shape the
architect chooses — so that editing the path in one place updates
both the installer and the stub simultaneously. The invariant is
"stub-points-at-hub"; the mechanism is shared state. Any other
arrangement — copy-paste string literals, helper-private defaults,
caller-supplied paths the two sides don't consult together — is a
design regression because it admits the possibility of drift.

**Independent failures, not cascade failures.** Each doc install is
its own best-effort operation. An unreadable canonical doc fails
that one install, surfaces in the per-file status as an error, and
leaves the other installs running. This mirrors how the parent's
init orchestrator already treats IDE configuration: a missing
optional dependency is a skip, not a run-ending exception. A
developer whose init produced four successful doc installs and one
failed one still has an almost-complete hub and a clear report of
what is missing; a developer whose init exploded on the first
failure has nothing at all.

## Good examples

A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time. The helper asks
initContent for the list of canonical methodology docs, receives
five names, and iterates them. For each name it asks initContent for
the doc's bytes, writes those bytes to the corresponding host-side
file under the shared doc-hub path, and reports `created`. It then
writes a small hub index file that lists each installed doc by name
with a relative link, reporting that as `created` too. The init
summary shows six `created` lines for this step (five docs plus the
index) alongside the methodology stub, the scaffolded slash commands,
and the MCP wiring. The developer opens the host-side hub folder and
sees five files whose contents are byte-identical to the docs the
maintainer edits in `docs/`, plus a hub index that links to each one.

A developer re-runs `aide_init` on an already-initialized project.
Every doc install checks the target, finds the file present, and
reports `exists`. The hub index reports `exists` as well. Nothing on
disk changed; the summary reads cleanly as "already done"; the
developer's time spent on the re-run was zero.

A developer has customized one of the installed methodology docs to
add their team's interpretation of a rule. They re-run init to pick
up a change to the MCP wiring. The helper sees the customized doc
in place and reports `exists`, leaving the customization intact. The
other four docs also report `exists`. The customization survived the
re-run by the same idempotency rule that protects scaffolded slash
commands.

A maintainer adds a new canonical methodology doc to `docs/` and
extends initContent's enumeration API to include it. They release a
new version of the server. On the next host-project init, this
helper iterates the updated list without any code change of its own,
installs the new doc alongside the five existing ones, and the hub
index now includes a link to the new doc. The change landed in one
file (initContent's registry); this helper picked it up for free;
the host project gained the new methodology surface without any
developer intervention beyond running init.

An agent session in a host project needs to scaffold a new `.aide`
file. Following the stub's pointer, it lands on the hub index, sees
a link to `aide-spec.md` and another to `aide-template.md`, and
reads only those two. It does not read `automated-qa.md` because
the current task is not a QA loop. The session spent the token budget
on the specific material its task needed, not on the full methodology,
which is exactly the progressive disclosure this helper was designed
to enable at the delivery layer.

## Bad examples

A helper that reads `docs/aide-spec.md` directly with `readFile`
"because it's one more line than calling into initContent". The
single-reader invariant has just been broken at this helper. A later
rename of `aide-spec.md` updates the initContent registry and breaks
this helper because nothing on this side is listening; the init run
on the next host project fails with a file-not-found error that
points at the wrong module.

A helper that hardcodes a local array of methodology doc names —
`["aide-spec", "aide-template", "progressive-disclosure",
"agent-readable-code", "automated-qa"]` — inside its own source. A
maintainer adds a sixth canonical doc to `docs/` and extends the
initContent registry. The registry propagates through writeMethodology
on the next build, but this helper silently continues installing five
docs, and the hub index silently continues listing five entries. The
new canonical doc exists in the registry and is invisible to host
projects. The enumeration invariant has been broken by the second
source of truth for what counts as "the methodology".

A helper that catches a read error on one canonical doc and aborts
the whole install, returning zero files written. The host project
has no methodology hub at all, the init summary shows a single
aggregate failure, and the developer has to debug the helper before
they can even read what *should* have been installed. Per-file
independence was the whole point of the design; skipping it traded
it for single-exception simplicity that doesn't belong in an
init tool.

A helper that detects an existing doc in the host hub, compares its
content against the canonical source, and overwrites it when the two
differ "because the canonical is authoritative". The developer's
customization is gone, they notice only when their next AIDE-related
task produces the wrong suggestion, and trust in the tool is broken.
The idempotency rule is "detect and skip", not "detect and
reconcile".

A helper that writes its doc hub to one path and a matching stub
that names a slightly different path — `aide/` versus `.aide/`,
trailing slash versus no trailing slash, forward slash versus
platform-native separator. The stub points at nothing; the agent
lands on a missing directory; the refactor technically shipped and
is technically broken. A shared path source would have caught this
at compile time; two coincident literals did not catch it at all.

A hub index file whose body reads "## The AIDE Methodology — read
these docs before writing any `.aide` file" with that sentence typed
inline in the helper. The sentence teaches an AIDE rule, so it is
canonical content, and it is living in this helper's source. The
doctrine invariant has been broken at the index. The fix is to
source the teaching sentence from canonical content through
initContent, or to remove it from the index entirely and leave the
index as a pure map of structure.
